WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.872
December 19

2
00:00:02.873 --> 00:00:05.831
Dear Daddy Longlegs,

3
00:00:05.832 --> 00:00:10.340
You never answered my question—are you bald?

4
00:00:10.341 --> 00:00:16.859
It's a very important question because I'm trying to draw a picture of you.

5
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:22.762
While I was drawing last night, Sallie came into my room.

6
00:00:22.763 --> 00:00:26.204
"Who's that?" she asked.

7
00:00:26.205 --> 00:00:29.176
"The kind old man who's sending me to college.

8
00:00:29.177 --> 00:00:33.117
It's difficult to draw him because I've never met him."

9
00:00:33.118 --> 00:00:37.188
"You've never met him, but he's sending you to college?

10
00:00:37.189 --> 00:00:40.096
That's very generous of him."

11
00:00:40.097 --> 00:00:42.373
"Yes, it is."

12
00:00:42.374 --> 00:00:48.366
I didn't look up because I didn't want her to ask any more questions about you—or me.

13
00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:52.058
So I concentrated on drawing your eyes. I

14
00:00:52.059 --> 00:00:56.270
decided to color them gray. "How

15
00:00:56.271 --> 00:00:59.014
do you know what color his eyes are?" Sallie

16
00:00:59.015 --> 00:01:02.447
stood behind me, watching. "I'm

17
00:01:02.448 --> 00:01:05.195
just guessing." I

18
00:01:05.196 --> 00:01:07.598
scribbled in your eyebrows. They

19
00:01:07.599 --> 00:01:10.860
stuck out like a porch roof. Sallie

20
00:01:10.861 --> 00:01:16.607
laughed. "Those are some eyebrows!" I

21
00:01:16.608 --> 00:01:23.960
laughed too. "I think they call them 'beetle brows' in novels." "I

22
00:01:23.961 --> 00:01:28.850
think they do." Sallie laughed again. But

23
00:01:28.851 --> 00:01:31.435
there was no laughter on your face. For

24
00:01:31.436 --> 00:01:36.716
your mouth, I drew a straight line that turned down at the corners. "He

25
00:01:36.717 --> 00:01:40.846
looks like a crabby old thing!" Sallie said. "Well,

26
00:01:40.847 --> 00:01:45.455
maybe he is. He never answers my letters." "That's

27
00:01:45.456 --> 00:01:48.671
rude!" Sallie said. "He's

28
00:01:48.672 --> 00:01:52.420
a kind but rude old man." Until

29
00:01:52.421 --> 00:01:55.733
this point I'd been drawing quickly. But

30
00:01:55.734 --> 00:02:00.745
when I reached the top of your head, I got stuck. "I

31
00:02:00.746 --> 00:02:04.945
can't decide whether he has white or black hair. Or

32
00:02:04.946 --> 00:02:09.570
maybe he has gray hair mixed with dark hair. What's

33
00:02:09.571 --> 00:02:15.505
that called? Oh, I know. Salt and pepper." "Or

34
00:02:15.506 --> 00:02:19.510
maybe he's bald!" Sallie said. "That's

35
00:02:19.511 --> 00:02:24.378
another possibility." I put down my pencil. "I

36
00:02:24.379 --> 00:02:29.216
have to stop drawing until I figure out what kind of hair he has." "How

37
00:02:29.217 --> 00:02:32.613
are you going to find that out?" Sallie asked. "I'll write

38
00:02:32.614 --> 00:02:35.405
him a letter, of course." So here

39
00:02:35.406 --> 00:02:39.436
I am writing you another letter about your hair. And now,

40
00:02:39.437 --> 00:02:45.008
it's not only me who wants to know its color; Sallie wants to know too. There was

41
00:02:45.009 --> 00:02:47.786
something else Sallie wanted to know. But it

42
00:02:47.787 --> 00:02:50.675
was about me, not you. "Every night

43
00:02:50.676 --> 00:02:56.516
you put a Do Not Disturb sign on your door," she said. "I'm lucky

44
00:02:56.517 --> 00:02:59.106
you were free tonight. What are

45
00:02:59.107 --> 00:03:03.538
you so busy doing in here?" "Oh, just

46
00:03:03.539 --> 00:03:07.478
studying." I rolled

47
00:03:07.479 --> 00:03:11.411
up my drawing and tucked it in a desk drawer. "You must

48
00:03:11.412 --> 00:03:14.965
really like to study!" Sallie said. The truth

49
00:03:14.966 --> 00:03:18.305
is, I'm not studying. I never

50
00:03:18.306 --> 00:03:23.254
study at night, no matter how many essays are due the next day. Instead I

51
00:03:23.255 --> 00:03:26.438
just read. There are

52
00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:32.550
so many novels that I've never read, and so many things that I don't know. The other

53
00:03:32.551 --> 00:03:39.464
girls seem to know everything while I have 18 blank years behind me. I'd never

54
00:03:39.465 --> 00:03:44.900
read Oliver Twist or Jane Eyre . I

55
00:03:44.901 --> 00:03:53.772
didn't know that King Henry VIII was married more than once or that Shelley was a poet. I

56
00:03:53.773 --> 00:04:01.347
didn't know that R.L.S. stood for Robert Louis Stevenson or that he wrote Treasure Island . I'd

57
00:04:01.348 --> 00:04:05.345
never seen a picture of the Mona Lisa .

58
00:04:05.346 --> 00:04:11.496
And you probably won't believe this, but I'd never heard of Sherlock Holmes either!

59
00:04:11.497 --> 00:04:14.957
Now I know all these things and a lot more.

60
00:04:14.958 --> 00:04:20.224
But you can see how I needed to catch up. And it's fun!

61
00:04:20.225 --> 00:04:24.468
All day I look forward to the evening.

62
00:04:24.469 --> 00:04:29.755
Then I put on my red bathrobe, sit on my couch, and read.

63
00:04:29.756 --> 00:04:32.020
One book isn't enough.

64
00:04:32.021 --> 00:04:34.872
I usually have four going at once.

65
00:04:34.873 --> 00:04:37.691
Right now I'm reading Little Women

66
00:04:37.692 --> 00:04:44.960
. When I discovered I was the only girl in the dorm who didn't know that book, I said

67
00:04:44.961 --> 00:04:50.626
nothing. I just went and bought it with $1.12 of my

68
00:04:50.627 --> 00:04:55.554
allowance. The Christmas holidays begin next

69
00:04:55.555 --> 00:05:00.379
week. Most of the girls are starting to pack their trunks to go

70
00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:07.191
home. The dormitory hallways are so full of trunks that you can hardly get past

71
00:05:07.192 --> 00:05:10.047
them. Everyone is bubbling over with

72
00:05:10.048 --> 00:05:12.869
excitement. Nobody is studying very

73
00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:19.291
much. I'm going to have a wonderful time, even though I'll be staying

74
00:05:19.292 --> 00:05:23.217
here. There's another freshman who isn't going

75
00:05:23.218 --> 00:05:27.109
home. She's from Texas, so it's too far to

76
00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:30.533
travel. We're planning to take long

77
00:05:30.534 --> 00:05:36.048
walks. And if there's any ice, we want to learn to skate.

78
00:05:36.049 --> 00:05:43.196
Then there's the whole library full of books—and three weeks with nothing to do but read!

79
00:05:43.197 --> 00:05:44.688
Good-bye, Daddy.

80
00:05:44.689 --> 00:05:47.951
I hope that you're feeling as happy as I am.

81
00:05:47.952 --> 00:05:49.125
Yours truly,

82
00:05:49.126 --> 00:05:51.864
Judy

83
00:05:51.865 --> 00:05:55.024
P.S. Don't forget to answer my question.

84
00:05:55.025 --> 00:06:00.326
If you don't want to answer, have your secretary send me a telegram.

85
00:06:00.327 --> 00:06:03.635
He can just say Mr. Smith is quite bald.

86
00:06:03.636 --> 00:06:06.119
Or Mr. Smith is not bald.

87
00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:09.445
Or Mr. Smith has white hair.

88
00:06:09.446 --> 00:06:15.284
And you can deduct the 25 cents for the telegram from my allowance.

89
00:06:15.285 --> 00:06:21.239
Good-bye until January—and have a merry Christmas!

